  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "Fouquart and Chen frame shadercoding as both GPU graphics technique and live digital-art practice. They explain fragment shaders, raymarching, distance fields, and mathematical image generation, then connect those techniques to Shader Showdown performance. The source argues that technical mastery and constraint can enable expressive real-time improvisation.",
    "summary_long": "Florine Fouquart and Chu-Yin Chen present shadercoding as a practice located at the junction of GPU programming, mathematics, computer graphics, and performance art. The article begins with a technical introduction: shaders are GPU programs that participate in rendering, fragment shaders map pixel positions to colors, and mathematical formulas can generate patterns, animation, and interactivity. Raymarching receives particular attention because it lets a fragment shader recover three-dimensional depth by casting rays through distance-field descriptions of space, making complex forms and repeated spaces available in real time. This technical exposition is not an end in itself. The authors use it to question the figure of the digital artist as a technical artist whose aesthetic choices are formed through scientific and mathematical processes. The article then shifts into a practice-based reflection on live coding, especially Shader Showdown events where participants write visual shader effects in 25 or 30 minutes before an audience. The source argues that constraint, visibility, and time pressure can become generative. Rather than separating technique from expression, the authorial account describes technical mastery as the condition that allows improvisation, bodily absorption, and dialogue with spectators. The strongest evidence is explanatory and experiential: a shader pipeline taxonomy, descriptions of fragment-shader and raymarching procedures, a concrete vector-math lighting example, the judging structure of Shader Showdowns, and a first-person account of reaching the Revision 2018 final. The result is a source best read as theoretical-practical commentary rather than empirical validation. It establishes a vocabulary for understanding shadercoding as art, but its general claims about performance rest on reflective experience, cited aesthetic theory, and examples from live-coding culture rather than systematic audience or practitioner study.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Technical Object Of Study",
        "body": "The source defines shadercoding through the shader itself: a GPU-directed program involved in the rendering pipeline. Its taxonomy of vertex, tessellation, geometry, and fragment shaders gives readers a concrete account of where code enters image production. This matters because the later art argument depends on treating code as the actual medium of visual formation, not as a hidden support tool.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
