      {
        "heading": "Fragment Shaders As Generators",
        "body": "The authors emphasize fragment shaders because they convert pixel positions into colors and can use formulas, time variables, and external inputs to generate animated images. That framing makes shadercoding legible as a generative practice: the image is computed from mathematical relations, and interaction can be incorporated into the same visual procedure.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "2",
          "0"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Raymarching And Spatial Depth",
        "body": "Raymarching is presented as the technique that lets fragment shaders recover 3D depth. The source explains the procedure in terms of distance functions, rays launched per pixel, stepwise traversal, and encounters with objects described by signed distance fields. This supports the claim that mathematical scene descriptions can produce complex real-time visual spaces.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "3",
          "4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Technique As Artistic Condition",
        "body": "The article repeatedly rejects a simple opposition between technical constraint and creative expression. Mathematical fluency, spatial reasoning, vector operations, and shader language mastery are treated as conditions of artistic agency. In this account, the artist gains expressive freedom not by avoiding technical systems, but by understanding them deeply enough to improvise inside them.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
